.TH FFTSTEST 1
.SH NAME
fftstest \- check ffts(2), the multivariate transform
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fftstest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fftstest
checks
.IR ffts (2)
against the direct sums and prints
.B OK
if everything agrees.
.B -v
prints each measured number.
.PP
The univariate case is checked against the
.RI O( n \u\s-22\s+2\d)
sum, in both sign conventions, at sixteen lengths.
The multivariate case is checked against a two-dimensional direct sum,
which is the only check that can see a wrong stride: a transform down
the columns when it should be along the rows returns perfectly good
numbers about the wrong array, and every one-dimensional check would
pass.
It is also checked that the two calls may be made in either order, which
the interface promises.
.PP
The interleaved form \- one array of alternating real and imaginary
parts, with the magnitude of
.I isn
set to 2 \- is checked against the separate-array form.
It agrees exactly, because it is the same arithmetic reached through
different index calculations, and those calculations are what is under
test.
.PP
Every length from 8 upward is a regression test.
The implementation this replaced indexed past the end of its argument
for every length of 8 or more and the process died; it passed for 2
through 7, and nothing in the tree ever called it.
.PP
Since the interface cannot report an error, the refusals are checked by
what does
.I not
happen: a shape that is not an array must leave the data untouched.
A good call is then checked to change it, or a routine that did nothing
at all would satisfy every refusal.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/fftstest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ffts (2),
.IR signal (2),
.IR signaltest (1)
.SH BUGS
A stride computed wrongly does not produce wrong numbers, it indexes off
the end of the array, and a Dis process that faults leaves
.I emu
waiting rather than exiting.
So that class of fault shows up as this program appearing to hang, with
.B "Broken: array bounds error"
on the standard error, and not as a
.B FAIL
line.
Run it under
.IR timeout (1)
or its equivalent if that matters.
.PP
The direct sums are quadratic in one dimension and quartic in two, so
the lengths checked are small: 128 in one dimension and 8 by 6 in two.
.PP
Only two-dimensional transforms are checked against a direct sum.
A three-dimensional transform is three calls of the same machinery, but
that it composes correctly at rank three is inferred and not measured.
